        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Sealing stamped concrete comes down to three things: a clean, completely dry surface, the right chemistry for what is already there, and two thin coats in the label's weather window. An acrylic film is the usual choice because it brings the color back; a penetrating sealer is the honest pick only if you want a natural look and better grip.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Put grit in the last coat wherever feet get wet, fix haze with a solvent re-wet before reaching for stripper, and reseal every two to three years before the antiquing wears away. For the products themselves, start with our stamped concrete sealer picks.
          </p>
